title: labels
---



```python
labels(data, own=None, ref=None)
```

Readable labels for the columns of a panel (or every field of a dataset id).

Variables take their metadata ``title``; columns written by ``geo.change()``
(``<var>_2024``, ``<var>_change``...) are labelled from their variable; group
columns (``department``) take the scale's label.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name   | Type                     | Description                                                                                                                                               | Default    |
|--------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| data   |                          | A panel (any DataFrame) or a dataset id                                                                                                                   | _required_ |
| own    | dict\[str, str\] \| None | Labels for columns of your own, ``{column: label}``. They are stored on the table, so the figures, tables and readings of the analysis functions use them | `None`     |

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type   | Description                                                                                                               |
|--------|--------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | dict   | ``{column: label}`` for the columns it knows. Pass it to Plotly: ``px.scatter(df, x=..., y=..., labels=geo.labels(df))``. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia
import geo4bolivia as geo
import plotly.express as px

df = geo.load("sdg07", variables=["sdg7_1_cae", "sdg7_1_elc"], years=2024)
px.scatter(df, x="sdg7_1_cae", y="sdg7_1_elc", labels=geo.labels(df),
           title="Electricity and clean cooking, ~2024")
```


